PB,

The best clue I can give you at a moment's notice is that the WAV
files are digital and the sound is fed from the motherboard thru to
the sound hardware interface on the riser card.  The signals for Audio
CDs follow a path direct from the CD-ROM drive thru an audio cable to
the riser card.

Activate the Control Panel, click on the system icon and look at all
the devices.  Are any of the devices preceded by either a yellow or a
red icon?  If so, these are not working properly.  While you're at it,
do the same for both IDE and SCSI drives, making a specific
comparision of the IRQs, ports, and other resources used by the ESS
sound subsystem.  You'll have to write this info down, because the
dunderheads in Redmond did not make it easy to print any of this
information out.  Duh!  Like who would EVER want to have printed
records of a system configuration.

If the sound works OK with the IDE disk and fails with the SCSI disk,
I'll guess that there is some sort of conflict between sound and the
SCSI host adapter.

That's the best I can do for now... Ben Myers

