Hi Folks,

Just a note to wish you all a Happy and Prosperous New Year and welcome
you all to the real start of the New Millennium. I know, that "midnight"
has crossed the meridian for many of you as I write this. Greetings,
belated though they may be.

I remain disappointed at the state and the future of the operating
system that has brought us all together. We've all made our individual
efforts to make it better. Please continue to support OpenVMS, and those
who work to sustain and improve it.

A lurker on this group wrote to me privately and made a few points
related to the millennium argument. It appears that there truly is a
number of "wrongs" that make a right, or at least an order of magnitude
which does. As he said, "convention" dictated that 1-Jan-2000 marked the
beginning of the new millennium, even though only 1999 years had passed
since the date declared as the start of time A.D. (a.k.a. "the Common
Era"). (The question of centuries never arose as a discussion topic.)

This same "convention", apparently, dictates that OpenVMS has, for all
practical intents and purposes, been relegated to the scrap pile.

So, we'll all be less productive from this point forward and will lose
much sleep thanx to viruses, blue screens and what-have-you. Though we
be separated, we'll always be joined by our common experiences with
OpenVMS.

It's been a pleasure to be associated with all of you. While I recognize
that many of you do not share that sentiment, I still value all of you
and your collective intelligence and experience. I've learned a lot, and
hope that I've given back some small measure of the value I've gained
from being here.

May the New Year bring you much joy, success and contentment.

May the New Millennium lead our progeny and theirs to a bright future
filled with hope and prosperity.

-- 
David J. Dachtera
