Hi N.N., 

argosy22@my-deja.com wrote:
> 
> Hi all,
> 
> I've just started a new position where we are running
> a number of hardware platforms.  One of them is Alpha
> on Compaq, True64.

"Tru64" without "e". 

> 
> When I log on, I get:
> Compaq Tru64 UNIX V5.1 (Rev. 732) (somewhere.com) (pts/2)

The versions are mostly called like "5.1 A" in discussions, 
but the software uses some number like you show (try "uname -a"). 
I would like to see some equivalence table on the machine 
(or software producing that "version letter" immediately ...). 

> 
> I'm familiar with Sun, and I am trying to run the
> same Sun commands that would show me info on the
> hardware, etc.  Ie.  dmesg, ndd, prtvtoc, sysdef,
> prtconf, prtdiag, hostconfig, mach, arch,
> showrev.  These commands would show me info about
> the OS, CPU, memory, disk drives, file systems, etc.

I do not know about the Sun specifics, but IMO all Unix variants 
differ when it comes to these issues, mostly because they have 
different approaches to disk / partition / file system handling. 
(On AIX you use "oslevel", "lsdev", "lspv", ...)

Disclaimer for the following: 
I still use version 4 - no info about version 5 specifics.


For Tru64, there is a tool "sys_check" that produces a single 
large HTML file containing loads of info about the machine, 
both hardware and software. Get it and try it: 
   ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/IAS/sys_check/sys_check.html 

The result has a table of contents and lots of internal links 
so that you can easily reach the item you want to see. 

It will probably contain more info than you need - I would 
like to see a version that accepts parameters so that I can 
_restrict_ the output generated. 

> 
> The only Sun commands that seem to work are:
> hostid, hostname, psrinfo, and they don't tell
> me what I want to know.
> 
> I'm trying to find a concise website of the commands
> that run on True64.  Using the man pages takes
> forever.   Does anyone know of such a site?

Try these: 
http://www.unix.digital.com/faqs/publications/best_practices/
http://www.UNIX.digital.com/faqs/publications/pub_page/pubs_page.html
http://www.UNIX.digital.com/faqs/publications/base_doc/DOCUMENTATION/HTML/BOOKSHELF.HTM

IMO, the Tru64 books are quite lengthy - easy reading, but 
long to get through, and you may miss the important details. 
For these, switch to "man". 


> 
> Or, can anyone give me a concise set of commands
> that will work?

IMO, all commands do work, but they may not be made for the 
intentions you have ;-) 

> 
> Also, when I have been searching for info, I find
> results on True64, alpha, and vms.  Are all these related?

Tru64 = the Unix variant on these machines. 
VMS = a different OS on these machines. 
Alpha = the name for the CPU in these machines. 

Regards, 
