Author: aaron spink <spink@pa.dec.com>


 "Erik Corry" <erik@arbat.com> wrote in message
 news:8t0s5i$5e41t$1@gjallar.daimi.au.dk...
 > How do you figure this?  A Rambus channel gives you 1066Mbyte/s to
 > 1600Mbyte/s (PC600-PC800), while a DDR channel gives you 1600Mbyte/s
 > or 2100Mbyte/s (200MHz or 266MHz).  I don't see a factor of 4 there.
 >

A rambus channel will give you 800 Mb/s/p while the best DDR will do is 266,
but probably only 200 on a large memory system.
  
Thats a factor of 4.  Now if you want to count over head of additional pins
besides data pins, I'd be happy to.  Take a look at the Intel BX, 815, or the
Via chipset specs and figure out the number of pins that are needed as
overhead.  In most cases for and SDRAM based memory bus you need more than 1
copy of the address and command pins because of the uneven loading compared to
the data pins.  Overall it comes to roughly 120 pins for a 64/72 bit SDRAM
interface and roughly 40 pins for a DRDRAM interface.  So for the same number
of pins you'll get roughly 4x the bandwidth with rambus as DDR in a full scale
memory system.
  
 > Well, no, it supports 32 devices per channel.  No rows, at least not per
 > channel.
 >

 When talking about multible channel designs, rows = devices.
  
 > Surely for a high end SDRAM design you can use 1 bit wide DRAM chips
 > and get 72 per SIMM, which makes at least 216 chips per channel (3 SIMMS)
 > as far as I can see?  But high end SDRAM designs use buffering, don't
 > they, so the limits are higher (and the latencies a little worse).
 >

Go find 1bit wide (DDR) SDRAM chips on the market.  When you do get me a price
quote.  The last company that I head of making them was IBM and as far as I can
tell, they don't make them anymore.  The min width seems to be 4 bits wide and
thats on special more expensive parts.  For volume manufacturing, 8bit wide
DRAMs seems to be the min.
  
The SDRAM address/command interface is good for 8-16 devices total.  Beyond
that you need buffering.  That means to support the same number of dram chips
as a 4 channel rambus interface, the SDRAM interface is going to need between
16-32 buffers.  Thats gonna make the latency real nice.
  
 > I don't know so much about this aspect of it, but at any rate I don't
 > think there is a 32 chip per channel limit for high end SDRAM designs.
 >

not per channel, per pin.  All that matters is the rows/pins.
  
