It's probably waiting for a spin-up command from the scsi host adapter,
before it starts running.

This is common on scsi drives, especially older ones as it helps to prevent
a power surge on startup.

I found mention that this is not configurable on the RZ56 drive.

http://www.aquascape.com/theref/hard_drives/layouts/hs_dec_rz5x.html



If it's really a problem, you might want to consider buying a newer drive.
I'd personally consider yourself lucky it's lasted this long as the old
5.25" full height DEC drives were not known for their longevity.





