In article <3A3A6C94.3837A792@srv.net>, Kevin Handy <kth@srv.net> writes:

  I'd encourage formally enlisting some help here, lest you steer 
  the client wrong...

:Currently they are limited to 8 serial lines, with a composite
:bandwidth of 9600 baud. They would like to add more lines to
:the remote site, but instead of using another Mux/leased line,
:I'd prefer to set up a ethernet bridge between the two buildings,
:possibly using the same leased line if possible.

  Trying to run an Ethernet across a 9600 baud datalink is going to be,
  um, problematic at best.

  You need to figure out the maximum bandwidth of this particular leased 
  line, and then (if it is sufficent) configure a pair of bridges or a 
  pair of switches or (if you are not bridging the LAN) a pair of network 
  routers between the sites.

  How far apart are these two sites?  (Depending on the distance and on
  the available bandwidth and the latency, you might be able to bridge
  the network segments.  Otherwise, you will probably end up using a
  protocol router...

  And since you mention LAT, you will further want to investigate what
  features you use if you decide to move to a routing-based solution,
  as the use of IP and telnet for remote access may not provide you with 
  a direct match for the features of LAT you are using.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
