Hi Michael

Glad to see that someone is still taking the time to resurrect a classic
like the 11/45.

Sure sounds like your machine is hung. One quick thing to check is the
Unibus signals ACLO and DCLO. They can be easily measured by multimeter
on the power connectors to the backplane, as they are the violet and
yellow colored cables. Alternately, find the Unibus pinout and measure
them directly on the bus. They are static signals in normal operation
and should be 3.5 volts or better.

If DCLO is asserted, odds are that you have a faulty regulator (H744,
H754 or H745). If ACLO is asserted, then the bet is on the +15 v
regilator card card that lives in the power supply rack underneath a
horizontal fan, at the front of the chassis containing the regulators.

Remember that any faulty regulator powering anything connected to the
bus can cause these signals to be asserted.

You can test for a bus grant problem by checking BUS SACK. If this is
asserted, the the bus will be hung, and the most likely combination is a
missing bus grant card in conjunction with an M9302 terminator. The M930
was too dumb to hang the bus.

Something else to ponder - on powering up, the PDP 11 will execute a
trap to 24 in memory (the powerfail/restart vector). As you have no
memory, the request will timeout and generate a trap to 4 (unibus
timeout trap). As you have no memory, this too will time out and so on
ad infinitum. As the CPU is busy doing things of a higher priority than
attending to the switches on the console, you won't get much response.
Even the halt key won't work, as the CPU is spinning its wheels in the
powerup microcode, and hasn't even thought about executing PDP11
instructions or entering the console microcode loop.

The module numbers for memory that you refer to sound like the mos and
bipolar memory options - very expensive when new and hence rare. The
11/45 typically used good old fashioned Unibus core memoy like the
MF11-UP memory set or similar, which should be a bit easier to find.

Hey I guess that training course I did about twenty five years ago was
better than I thought. Then again, the '45 CPU is more or les the same
as that of the mighty 11/70, so had an extraordinarily long lifespan.

Good luck!!!

