Lisa,

Here are the risks associated with doing a flash BIOS upgrade:

1. You make a major clerical error, and apply the wrong flash BIOS
update.  This makes the board useful for electronic scrap only.
2. There is a power outage while the flash BIOS update program is
running.  Your computer shuts down.  More electronic scrap.
3. The flash BIOS chip has been updated as many times as it designed
to be updated, and you update it just one more time.  The board is
toast, but it was probably going to be pretty soon anyway.  Windows
95/98/NT/2000 all update the non-volatile RAM all too regularly, as
part of the wonderful Windows Plug-and-Play.  This wears out more
motherboards than anything you or I would be likely to do.

In short, be careful when you update a motherboard BIOS.  Take
reasonable precautions, and the risk is small.  But, then I'm a BIOS
hacker, developing BIOS change procedures that the manufacturer never
envisioned, e.g. substituting an English-language BIOS for a Japanese
one.

... Ben Myers

