aw@mail1.bet1.puv.fi wrote:

>benmyers@ultranet.com (Ben Myers) wrote:
>> 1. You make a major clerical error, and apply the wrong flash BIOS
>> update.  This makes the board useful for electronic scrap only.
>> 2. There is a power outage while the flash BIOS update program is
>> running.  Your computer shuts down.  More electronic scrap.
>
>No, there's still hope - at least if you backed up the original
>BIOS to a file prior to flashing, or if you can otherwise find a
>compatible BIOS.  The proper way to fix it is to use an EEPROM
>programmer, but if you have a sufficiently compatible working
>motherboard, you can use that as a substitute for an EEPROM
>programmer.
>

You're right.  There's still hope.  But most people do not have access
to an EEPROM programmer.  Most computers these days have a soldered-on
flash BIOS chip, including many later DEC models.  So de-soldering a
BIOS chip, reprogramming it, re-soldering it?  Sounds like very little
hope for the average person.

One other remote possibility I forgot to mention.  If someone flashes
the wrong BIOS onto a board or loses power while flashing, as long as
the BIOS boot block area is not destroyed, DEC provided the software
to make a BIOS recovery diskette.  You have to make the recovery
diskette BEFORE attempting to update the BIOS.

Last of all, for people who do manage to wipe out the BIOS on their
DEC Wintel motherboard, I have a limited supply of BIOS EEPROM chips
for Celebris XL and Prioris XL systems, and spare motherboards for
some other models...  


