I'm not sure how it is in Finland, but here in the U.S.A., the average
computer shop is not professional enough to do a BIOS operation like
you've described.  They'll only tell you it is too bad that the board
is useless, and maybe, just maybe, try to find a replacement board.
Or sell you another system.  

Even if they are professional enough to have the tools and knowledge
to do the job, it is not worth their time to do so, or you would not
want to pay the hourly rate needed to compensate a first-rate tech to
have it done.

Really, even with a socketed and removable BIOS chip, getting a
replacement BIOS chip programmed by a competent person with the
requisite equipment is VERY unlikely.

Frankly, even the major name brand players rarely repair individual
boards any more, except maybe under the duress of a poorly negotiated
contract with a government, requiring spare parts for 10 years.
Instead, they scavenge the landscape for replacement parts, which may
be new or may be pulls from retired equipment.

This is all the result of computers becoming commodity products, like
toasters and microwave ovens, rather than highly specialized and not
widespread equipment as in days gone by... Ben Myers

