The hot-pluggable aspect isn't an issue.  The real SRM issues are in picking
the "base" keyboard/mouse that will be used for the console.  And then
passing control between the OS and the console.  Today it's kind of hacky,
but not too complex to pass the KB back and forth - stopping an entire bus
is another proposition.  So... there will be more restrictions on the
ability to use "continue" if you halt with a USB keyboard as the console
keyboard.  Most likely all you will be able to do is CRASH or reboot.

>>
>>   The plug gets hot because of a current overflow on the USB, right? :-)
>>
>>   Seriously, USB will fix some problems.  I am also certain that USB will
>>   also bring along its share of new and unusual problems and interesting
>>   anomolies -- I've already seen a few...
>
>Oh yeah... And all this misses the fundamental point. If the mouse/keyboard
>interface was on the video card, then you are all set for multi-server
>configs. Plus the box is 'auto configuring'.


Back in the QDSS/QVSS days with the real Qbus device, which had
mouse/keyboard ports, multi-seat was possible.  Since then there has been no
more-or-less standard way to do this.  You will probably see multi-seat make
a come back with USB - I've already done some preliminary design for it.  It
is unlikely you will ever see the graphics makers start putting kb/mouse
ports onto their boards.








