Dennis Grevenstein <dennis@pcde.inka.de> writes:

>Hi,

>What do you think about the usability of a DEC 3000 alpha?

they're big. and they chew a fair bit of power. and generate
significant heat.  if none of this bothers you, they're quite
a nice workstation to use still.  you haven't said what
kind of dec3000.. the bottom end is the dec3400 (133Mhz 21064)
or even the 3300 (100Mhz 21064?).

if you have the hardware or are getting it really cheap, or
have a particular software requirement (you can't run VMS on
a PeeCee..) then it's really nice hardware.  if you're after
something `modern' and upgradeable, i wouldn't bother.  you
probably want to skip turbochannel and get a PCI based alpha.

>Of course I know the specs and what operating systems can
>be run an such a box, but there are some things I am
>currently thinking about: support by True64 Unix and OpenVMS.

tru64 is upported upto version 5.0.  support has been dropped
from either 5.0A or 5.1 onwards.  AFAIK OpenVMS continues to
support the hw, but i'm not a vms admin.

>Are there any decent(somewhat fast 24bit) graphic cards
>available for turbo-channel and supported by OpenVMS and
>True64? Up to which DEC Unix/True64 or OpenVMS release?

sure. the PMAGD is a dual plane, single slot card. it was quite
a nice card when i was using it, back in the 3.2C/dec unix 4.0
days.  there are also various PXG cards which give you 24bit
support under various releases.. some dropped support after 3.2
and others were still supported upto 4.0, but dropped in 5.0.


>Are there any 100mbit ethernet cards for DEC3000 machines?

nope. i've never heard of any 3rd party cards either.  your only
bet if you really need 100Mbit is to get a TC FDDI card and connect
it to a FDDI ring, which you then interface into a 100Mbit network.

not pretty.

>Are they supported by modern operating systems?

yep.  NetBSD 1.5 runs just fine on this hardware and it's as modern
as you can get.


cheers,

-jason


