A Digital KBD01-AA has been kicking around my parts stash for nearly four
years now since I paid $2 for it at MIT.  I thought I had gotten rid of it
a long time ago until it showed up behind a box on a shelf.  Until this
afternoon, it had never been opened or powered up.  The sticker still
sealed the opening over the power connector.

It's a small steel cabinet about 4"H x 15"W x 14"L.  The front panel has
four positions selectable by keyswitch, Program, Local, Remote/Secure,
Remote.  The rear has a DB-25 male connector, apparently RS-232, and a
DC-37 female connector.

Opening the cabinet reveals what is apparently a mini-QBus with three
dual-height slots occupied by, top to bottom, M7366, an unlabelled card
connected to the front panel, and M7364.  The M7366 has an 8085
microprocessor.  The Field Guide knows not of these modules.

Powering the unit while connected to a 9600bps console shows no output,
and no response to input.  An rs-232 checker connected to the DB-25 shows
the expected signals.  Altering the position of the keyswitch with a
paperclip (of course I don't have the matching key) shows no change in
function other than changing the front panel indicator lamps.

Any ideas?  Minimal available information indicates that this is a remote
diagnostic port for a PDP-11.  I have an LSI-11/73, can the KBD01-AA do
any useful tricks?  Can I put other dual-height QBus modules in this
cabinet?

-- 
Alexander_R_Svirsky_____________________________atlas@world.std.com


