I look after a network of DEC (Compaq) Alpha machines, running Tru64 UNIX,
versions V4.0d and above. I have recently investigated the newer releases
V5.0A and V5.1, but found a problem that prevents me from using them: the
late-version machine cannot write files to NFS-mounted directories. Large
files fail more often than smaller ones, but I get quite reliable failures
with just a few hundred kB, e.g. with the command something like

  cp /usr/dict/words /NFSmounted/file

(and am almost assured of a failure with a file of 5MB or so).


I get problems when the client is running V5.0A (with or without patch
t64v50aas0001-20000718.tar installed), or if it is running V5.1 (with patch
t64v51as0001-20001114.tar installed). The server can be either V4.0D or
V5.0 (I do not have a V5.0A or V5.1 server to test). There is no problem
when the client is pre-V5.0A.

When the client is V5.0A (and the server V5.0 or V4.0D), I get errors
(reported on the tty), and in the two files /var/adm/messages and
/var/adm/syslog.dated/*/kern.log get many lines like
  Aug 14 11:21:19 venice vmunix: NFS3 RFS3_WRITE failed for server milan: RPC: Server can't decode arguments
