>How does that go for thermal stress? I know it was a problem
>for the 064. Why not socket the CPU, cache, and SROM? It would be a TINY
>increase in space, but make the system upgradable and more servicable.

Those 1U x86 systems you've seen have extremely low-profile fan/heatsinks
on them in order to clear the lid of the case.  Such a fan/sink would almost
certainly Just Not Work for a 21264 using current process technology.  The
only way to get a taller heatsink/fan is to... you guessed it... lose the
socket's height by soldering the chip to the motherboard.

Could CPQ design a large flat passive heatsink with an adjacent blower that
forced a high volume of air over it?  Maybe.  But that'd use a great deal of
space inside the already tightly packed case, and raise costs even more.
Not an easy box to get out of any way except by a process shrink that cuts
power consumption, and they've been using those shrinks to keep up on MHz
instead, so you pretty much lose no matter what you do.

