>instead, so you pretty much lose no matter what you do.

I've always thought it would be interesting to build 1U systems with
chimney ventilation.  Essentially take a 6 inch drill bit and bore a hole
through the case and motherboard centered where the CPU is to sit.  Then
put in a redesigned motherboard that has a big round hole in it that the
CPU unit projects into.    There would also be a mandatory 1U size fan unit
at the bottom which would shove cold air up the chimney which would result
when the grills were aligned in the rack. Hot air wants to rise - so let
it!  Admittedly such a system is only really useful if you have a lot
of 1U systems stacked, but then, isn't that the whole point of 1U systems?

Or why not mount the 1U systems vertically instead of horizontally?  Then 
the heat can rise off the CPU naturally instead of being trapped against
it by the case.

The other option is to just forget about the 1U form factor (which is
really quite a silly shape in a number of ways) and sell instead a 2U or 3U
form factor system with a matching number of processors in it.  Even if
they are on small separate motherboards (not SMP) the geometric constraints
are not nearly as severe as within the 1U case. 

