In article <86d7faox9w.fsf@cartero.kjsl.com>, Javier Henderson <javier@kjsl.com> writes:
:	I'm trying to find what's the size limit for disk drives on an
:AlphaStation 255/300 with VMS 7.2. I currently have two 4 GB drives,
:both of which have been spinning for entirely too long and are
:starting to log too many errors for comfort.

  The OpenVMS maximum disk size limit is circa 1 TB, as per the information 
  in the OpenVMS FAQ.

  Since SCSI itself is more a standard collection of optionally-implemented 
  features than it is a standard, it is impossible to say if any particular 
  SCSI widget will work.  Some will, some will not.  Worse, a few SCSI 
  devices might appear to work and will later fail under load or will fail 
  under some odd sequence of SCSI commands, or will fail and lock up the 
  bus.  (My experience testing SCSI configurations over the years has shown 
  some really creative failure modes, too.)

  SCSI-3 updates went into V7.1-2 and later and into an ECO for V6.2 -- due 
  to a limitation in the mode page field for the disk size, the SCSI-2 spec 
  does not permit disks over a particular capacity (circa 16 GB, 16777215?,
  IIRC) though some vendors did find creative ways to avoid this.  One of 
  the most common: recent OpenVMS releases now have SCSI drivers that zero 
  the medium type byte and assume it to be the part of the disk size field, 
  even though the field is not part of the disk size field per the SCSI-2 
  specifications.  SCSI-3 uses a longword for the disk size field, rather
  than the existing 2 (or sometimes 3) bytes in earlier implementations.

  Once you get over the basic hurdle of "it works", the next problem with 
  these larger disks will be cooling -- I would not assume that larger (and 
  particularly hotter) disks will work within the AlphaStation 255 series 
  enclosure nor in any enclosure that lacks sufficient cooling for these 
  (hotter) disks.  The typical failure mode reported by folks that have 
  tried, um, cooking their disks will be correct operation followed (at 
  some time later) by increasing numbers of disk errors.

:	Any idea if these would work on this beast? I BTFW (browsed the
:fine website) at Compaq but didn't find anything relevant...

  Please take a look at discussions in the OpenVMS Ask The Wizard area such 
  as 4467, 4409, and 2048.  There are probably a few others.

  That said, the only widgets that are officially supported are those that 
  were tested (successfully) with the particular hardware configuration.
  These are obviously also the configurations that tend to be documented.

  If you can figure out how to explain this more simply -- well, explain it
  better than the simplistic "try it and see" :-) -- then please let me know 
  and I will add the text to the FAQ...  I'll see if I can come up with some
  text based on the various previous "try it and see" discussions...

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




